WEBVTT

00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:11.240
<v Sarah Okafor>Morning, everyone. Thursday the eighth. Are we all on? I can see seven little boxes, which is promising.</v>

00:00:12.289 --> 00:00:16.489
<v Marcus Hale>Morning. Absolutely filthy weather out there. Came across the car park sideways.</v>

00:00:17.070 --> 00:00:19.170
<v Callum Reid>That's just how you walk, mate.</v>

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:28.609
<v Marcus Hale>Cheers. Supposed to be going walking at the weekend, but if this keeps up, my weekend plans are becoming a pub lunch.</v>

00:00:29.910 --> 00:00:37.609
<v Priya Raman>Talking of the car park, whose new car is that by the gate? Very shiny. Bit brave parking it under that tree.</v>

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:42.630
<v David Whitfield>Not mine. Mine's the one where the dirt's doing structural work.</v>

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:47.189
<v Hannah Price>Protective coating. Sounds better on the paperwork.</v>

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:54.219
<v Sarah Okafor>Right, before we start certifying the cars—where are we with the bits already finished?</v>

00:00:55.789 --> 00:01:01.039
<v Olumide Adeyemi>Yeah, so I tested the site water yesterday and it's all fine now. That's done.</v>

00:01:01.829 --> 00:01:05.329
<v Hannah Price>Lovely. That's a completed item, then, not another sample outstanding?</v>

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:09.030
<v Olumide Adeyemi>Correct. Nothing outstanding on that one.</v>

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:16.140
<v Sarah Okafor>And I signed off the inspection on the relief valve on Tuesday. That inspection's closed.</v>

00:01:17.349 --> 00:01:21.549
<v David Whitfield>Yeah, the inspection and the work risk assessment are separate bits, though.</v>

00:01:21.980 --> 00:01:28.280
<v Sarah Okafor>Exactly. Davey, can you update the risk assessment for work on the Boiler Safety Relief Valve this afternoon?</v>

00:01:30.049 --> 00:01:35.650
<v David Whitfield>Yep, I'll update the risk assessment for work on the Boiler Safety Relief Valve this afternoon.</v>

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:40.280
<v Hannah Price>Fine. Just the work risk assessment, not reopening that inspection.</v>

00:01:41.370 --> 00:01:45.920
<v David Whitfield>That's it. The inspection stays signed off. It's the assessment wording I'm updating.</v>

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:49.260
<v Sarah Okafor>Good, thanks.</v>

00:01:50.079 --> 00:01:57.780
<v Olumide Adeyemi>Couple of observations from me. Saw the pressure on the palletiser dip for a few minutes. It came back on its own.</v>

00:01:58.549 --> 00:02:02.049
<v Callum Reid>Sorry, you clipped there. Came back without anyone touching it?</v>

00:02:02.519 --> 00:02:07.420
<v Olumide Adeyemi>Yeah, without intervention. Just reporting what I saw, not raising a job off it.</v>

00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:14.000
<v Olumide Adeyemi>And I noticed the line 1 was running a bit warm on nights, but it settled down.</v>

00:02:14.569 --> 00:02:16.669
<v Hannah Price>Settled before the end of nights?</v>

00:02:17.639 --> 00:02:21.139
<v Olumide Adeyemi>Yeah. Again, just an observation. No outstanding action from that.</v>

00:02:21.919 --> 00:02:28.569
<v Marcus Hale>On the plant side, still waiting on parts for the compressor one. Nothing we can do until they arrive.</v>

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:34.919
<v David Whitfield>Right, so that's still blocked rather than something we're picking up this morning.</v>

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:37.430
<v Marcus Hale>Exactly. Same position as before, unfortunately.</v>

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:43.330
<v Callum Reid>And the shutdown on the chiller on three is cancelled now. Production need it running.</v>

00:02:43.939 --> 00:02:47.090
<v Sarah Okafor>Right, cancelled, not just shuffled later in the day?</v>

00:02:48.949 --> 00:02:50.699
<v Callum Reid>Cancelled. It's off the list.</v>

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:58.860
<v Priya Raman>Um, I'll write up the near miss at the FLTs. That's fairly important, so I'll take that one.</v>

00:02:59.349 --> 00:03:01.800
<v Hannah Price>Yeah, the write-up's the bit still missing.</v>

00:03:02.219 --> 00:03:06.770
<v Priya Raman>Exactly. I'll do the write-up, rather than leave it as a verbal account.</v>

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:11.819
<v Sarah Okafor>Thanks, Priya Raman. Got you against the near miss at the FLTs.</v>

00:03:12.409 --> 00:03:19.409
<v Sarah Okafor>We also need someone to check the guarding on the line 2 robot. Top priority please. Who can take that?</v>

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:22.259
<v Callum Reid>I can't commit to taking that one.</v>

00:03:23.139 --> 00:03:26.289
<v David Whitfield>No, I can't put my name against it either.</v>

00:03:28.509 --> 00:03:36.210
<v Sarah Okafor>Right... nobody taking it, then. The guarding check on the line 2 robot stays unallocated, but it is still top priority please.</v>

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:43.710
<v Hannah Price>Yeah, don't let the lack of a name make it look like it's been dropped.</v>

00:03:45.719 --> 00:03:48.169
<v Sarah Okafor>Agreed. Needed, unowned. Not marked as covered.</v>

00:03:48.599 --> 00:03:55.599
<v Sarah Okafor>Priya Raman, could you sort the training records for the new starters after lunch, if you've got a spare minute?</v>

00:03:57.330 --> 00:04:03.280
<v Priya Raman>Sorry, no, I can't take the records as well. I haven't got that spare bit of time.</v>

00:04:03.939 --> 00:04:11.639
<v Sarah Okafor>Okay. Olumide, can you take that instead—sort the training records for the new starters after lunch, if you've got a spare minute?</v>

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:20.310
<v Olumide Adeyemi>Yeah, I'll take that. I'll sort the training records for the new starters after lunch, if I've got a spare minute.</v>

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:24.660
<v Hannah Price>That's the spare-minute one, then, not something we're calling urgent?</v>

00:04:26.269 --> 00:04:28.019
<v Sarah Okafor>That's right. Keeping that distinction.</v>

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:35.370
<v Marcus Hale>And I'll chase the purchase order for the line three. I'll take that one.</v>

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:39.490
<v Callum Reid>Was that tomorrow at 9:30?</v>

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:46.019
<v Marcus Hale>Yeah, tomorrow at 9:30. Not an emergency but don't let it slip—that's how I'm treating it.</v>

00:04:48.399 --> 00:04:53.300
<v Sarah Okafor>Thanks, Marcus Hale. Purchase order for the line three, tomorrow at 9:30, with you.</v>

00:04:53.649 --> 00:04:54.850
<v Marcus Hale>Yep, agreed.</v>

00:04:55.680 --> 00:05:02.680
<v Hannah Price>Sorry, just caught the word three over the fan noise. That's the purchase order, separate from the cancelled chiller shutdown?</v>

00:05:04.490 --> 00:05:08.339
<v Marcus Hale>Yeah, the purchase order for the line three. Not the shutdown.</v>

00:05:10.689 --> 00:05:14.889
<v Callum Reid>Definitely separate. The shutdown on the chiller on three is still cancelled.</v>

00:05:16.089 --> 00:05:18.889
<v Sarah Okafor>Okay. That covers the list. Anything I've misheard?</v>

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:24.550
<v David Whitfield>No, all clear here. Although someone sounds like they're sitting inside a hoover.</v>

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:28.480
<v Priya Raman>I thought that was your car's protective coating coming off.</v>

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:36.189
<v Olumide Adeyemi>Ha. It's this headset picking up every fan in the building. You all sounded clear to me.</v>

00:05:38.019 --> 00:05:40.120
<v Sarah Okafor>Right, we're good then. Thanks, everyone.</v>

00:05:41.069 --> 00:05:42.269
<v Marcus Hale>Cheers, all.</v>
